#Frontend Cheat Sheet
1. System and Project Initialization (The Big Bang)
These commands are typically run only once when setting up the environment or initializing a new project.

npm install -g pnpm

What it does: Installs the pnpm package manager globally on your machine. (Requires Node.js to be installed first).

pnpm create vite frontend --template react-ts

What it does: Initializes a brand new, empty React project with TypeScript configuration inside a folder named "frontend", using Vite as the build tool.

pnpm install (or simply: pnpm i)

What it does: Reads the package.json file and downloads all required dependencies into the node_modules folder. You must also run this whenever a teammate (or Git pull) updates the project with new packages.

2. Daily Development Workflow
These are the most frequently used commands during active development.

pnpm run dev

What it does: Starts the local development server (usually at http://localhost:5173). It watches your files and instantly updates the browser when you save changes (HMR - Hot Module Replacement).

pnpm run build

What it does: Prepares the application for Production. It compiles the TypeScript code into minified, browser-friendly JavaScript and bundles everything into a dist (distribution) folder. This is the folder you deploy to Azure or any static web host.

pnpm run preview

What it does: After running a build, this command spins up a local web server to test the generated production code. It allows you to verify that the minified application behaves exactly as it will in the cloud.

3. Adding Packages (Libraries)
Using pnpm add allows us to pull in new tools and UI components. These are the core libraries we added to our stack:

pnpm add @mui/material @emotion/react @emotion/styled

What it does: Installs Google's Material UI component library (buttons, cards, dialogs). The Emotion packages are required for MUI's internal CSS-in-JS styling engine.

pnpm add @mui/icons-material

What it does: Installs the official Material Design icons (e.g., the trash can and pencil icons used in our lists).

pnpm add react-router-dom

What it does: Installs the client-side router, enabling seamless navigation between logical pages (Dashboard, Properties, Tenants) without triggering a full browser page reload.

pnpm add @tanstack/react-query

What it does: Installs the server-state management library. It handles data fetching, built-in caching, automatic retries, and background UI updates.

4. Troubleshooting & Firefighting
When the "Ghost in the Machine" acts up, these commands will save the day:

Stop-Process -Name node -Force (Run in PowerShell)

What it does: Ruthlessly kills all running JavaScript/Node.js background servers. Highly useful if the Vite server gets stuck in the background and you get a "Port 5173 is already in use" error.

The "Nuke and Pave" approach (Fixing broken dependencies):

Delete the node_modules folder entirely.

Run pnpm install again.
(This is the frontend equivalent of "Have you tried turning it off and on again?" when the package manager gets confused).